Remember the trend:

shorter, more controlled heat pulses can help fabs get useful material changes while protecting an increasingly tight thermal budget.

8. Why temperature uniformity matters

A 300mm wafer is large relative to the nanometer-scale features being built on it.

The fab cannot have one side of the wafer meaningfully hotter than the other.

If temperature differs across the wafer, dopant activation and diffusion can also differ.

Then transistors near the center may behave differently from transistors near the edge.

That hurts uniformity and potentially yield.

So thermal-processing equipment must control:

- absolute temperature;
- temperature ramp rate;
- time at temperature;
- cooling rate;
- across-wafer uniformity;
- wafer-to-wafer repeatability.

This is similar to what we learned in deposition and ion implantation.

The headline process sounds simple.

The moat lies in controlling it precisely across millions of repeated manufacturing cycles.

9. Where Applied Materials fits

Applied Materials, ticker AMAT, has a broad thermal-processing portfolio.

Its anneal systems include lamp-, laser- and heater-based approaches designed for different combinations of temperature, time and device sensitivity.

Applied's Vantage Astra dynamic surface anneal system is a laser-based millisecond anneal tool.

The company positions these technologies around challenges such as lower thermal budgets, leakage control, interface quality and advanced-node scaling.

This fits Applied's broader business model.

Applied is not a pure-play thermal company.

It participates across many wafer-fabrication steps, including deposition, materials engineering, implant and thermal processing.

That breadth can matter because process steps interact.

A change in transistor materials can create a new deposition requirement, which changes the thermal recipe, which changes defect behavior and metrology needs.

Applied can participate across several of those transitions.

10. Where Tokyo Electron fits

Tokyo Electron, or TEL, also participates in thermal processing.

Its TELINDY family is a high-volume 300mm batch furnace platform used for oxidation, anneal and LPCVD.

Its newer EVAROS thermal-processing platform is also designed for 300mm batch processing with an emphasis on precise process control and productivity.

This highlights an important distinction:

Applied is especially prominent in single-wafer rapid and millisecond thermal processing.

TEL has strong batch-furnace exposure.

Different thermal problems can favor different tool architectures.

There is no ASML-style single supplier controlling all semiconductor heating.

11. Where ASM fits

ASM International is best known today for technologies such as atomic layer deposition and epitaxy, but it also sells vertical furnace systems.

Vertical furnaces can handle high-productivity batch thermal processes including diffusion and oxidation.

Again, the market is fragmented by application.

The question is not:

Who owns "heat"?

The better question is:

Which supplier has the best tool and process position for a specific thermal step that becomes more difficult as devices scale?

That is how you should analyze most semiconductor-equipment categories outside true monopolies such as EUV.

12. Annealing is used for more than implant damage

Today's core example is dopant activation after implantation.

But thermal processing appears throughout semiconductor manufacturing.

Heat can be used to:

- repair crystal defects;
- activate dopants;
- grow or modify oxide layers;
